          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              The explicit formulation of the principles of category theory … is still in need of improved axiomatization. I will be overjoyed when some young person responds to that need. (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Law16"} 2016
          %p#IsInABroadSense
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %strong elementary theory of the category of categories
            %em ETCC
            for short, is a name for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            axiomatizing the metacategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} CAT
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            that forms the intuitive background for naive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            with a view to the categorical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the analogous first-order axiomatization of the metacategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} SET
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %p
            Historically, the first attempt to formulate such a system of axioms was undertaken by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} F. W. Lawvere
            in his dissertation (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere63"}> Lawvere 63
            ) and a subsequent publication (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere66"}> Lawvere 1966
            ), and it is this system that is sometimes referred to as
            %em the
            ETCC. We will discuss it together with subsequent attempts to repair its technical flaws under this heading in the next section.
          %p
            Taking into account that the intuitively given category of categories is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} n-categorical perspective
            one would prefer to axiomatize CAT directly as a 2-category, and the resulting
            = succeed "," do
              %em elementary theory of the 2-category of categories
            = succeed "," do
              %strong ET2CC
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ET2CC"} section
          %h2#ETCC ETCC: Lawvere (1963) and beyond
          %blockquote
            %p
              In 1964, F. W. Lawvere proposed to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} found
              mathematics on the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+categories"} category of categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Tarski"} Alfred Tarski
              objected: “But what is a category if not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              together with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of
              = succeed "?\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p In the early 60s, Lawvere launched an ambitious long-term project to replace membership-based mathematics by more invariant concepts in order to arrive at a renewal of analysis and geometry adapted for the needs of modern (continuum) physics.
          %p
            His 1963
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} dissertation
            takes issue with set-theoretic notions at various levels, e.g. redefining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+categories"} comma categories
            in order to avoid reference to Hom-sets, but the most radical step is the attempt at a first-order axiomatization of the category of categories that he proposed to bypass
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            completely at a foundational level.
          %p The system of axioms that he published in a more elaborate form in 1966 is layered in three parts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em
                  elementary theory of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} abstract categories
                (ETAC) has, besides equality, three primitive predicates for domain, codomain, and composition and, as axioms, the usual axioms for identity, associativity, and so on. (See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+categories"} theory of categories
                for a variant of such an axiomatisation!)
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em basic theory
                (BT) adds axioms for, e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 2
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 3
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ≅
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^1\cong A^2
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subcategory"}> density
                  \-adequacy
                of the simple categories like
                %strong 1
                etc. within the metacategory.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em stronger theory
                (ST) then adds two further axioms to the basic theory concerning existence of completions and an ‘inaccessible’ category (p.18).
          %p
            As pointed out by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Isbell"} J. Isbell
            in 1967, one of Lawvere’s results (namely, the theorem on the ‘construction of categories by description’ on p.14) was mistaken, which left the axiomatics with insufficient power to construct models for categories. Several ways to overcome these problems were suggested in the following, but no system achieved unanimous approval: cf. Blanc-Preller(1975), Blanc-Donnadieu(1976), Donnadieu(1975), and McLarty(1991).
          %p
            As the ETCC also lacked the simplicity of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            in the attention of the research community that generally preferred to hedge their foundations with appeals to Gödel-Bernays set-theory or Grothendieck universes.
          %p
            Nevertheless, the axiomatic treatment of 1963/66 also contained the seminal idea to define discrete set-theoretic ‘foundations’ internally within a bigger category, and that would later on play a central role in Lawvere’s approach to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
          %h2#ET2CC ET2CC: an elementary theory of the 2-category of categories
          %p For Lawvere, ETCS and ETCC had a different status. The former provides abstract constant sets as convenient carriers for structured objects, whereas the latter provides the overall ontology of concepts, the intellectual working space.
          %p
            However, from an
            = succeed "," do
              %strong n-categorical perspective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the former. This suggests to posit an abstract concept of ‘foundation’ that, at the level of ETCS, shows up as set theory, gets refined to ‘category theory’ one step higher, then as 2-category theory, and so on. In addition, from this perspective it is more natural to axiomatize not the
            %em category
            of categories, but the
            %em 2-category
            of categories.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#HM25"} Hughes and Miranda
            have axiomatized an
            %em elementary theory of the 2-category of small categories
            (ET2CSC), and proven that a 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            satisfies these axioms if and only if it is of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(E)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            in some category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            that satisfies
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Exactness and projectivity conditions due to
                %a{:href => "#Bourke"} Bourke
                that ensure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                is of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(E)
                for a category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                with pullbacks:
              %ol
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                  has
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powers"} powers
                  by the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                  has quotients of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-congruences"} 2-congruences
                %li Codescent morphisms are effective.
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+objects"} discrete objects
                  are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
                %li Every object admits a codescent morphism from a projective object.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed"} well-pointed
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                has a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} classifier
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+morphism"} fully faithful
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                satisfies a suitable 2-categorical form of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            These axioms are called an elementary theory of the 2-category of
            %em small
            categories because they do not include an
            %em object
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            playing the role of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/michaelshulman/show/classifying+discrete+opfibration"} classifying discrete opfibration
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Weber07"} Weber
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
          %p
            Given this, one may hope to use finite 2-categorical limits and the “internal logic” to construct all the usual concrete categories out of this object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            Such a level of
            %em discreteness
            seems to be pervasive in this context. For instance, the approach of
            %a{:href => "#Przybylek14"} Przybylek
            to the concept of internal 2-cartesian closure takes discreteness as a starting point.
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %p
            R. F. C. Walters and R. Street had already conceived of their work on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structure"} Yoneda structures
            in the early 1970s as a 2-dimensional version of ETCC (cf. Walters
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#W71"} 1971
